         <title><![CDATA[Ontario cancer patients speak out over disappointment with drug funding decision]]></title>
         <link>https://www.cbc.ca/news/canada/toronto/ohip-out-of-country-coverage-denial-cancer-treatment-9.7389736?cmp=rss</link>
         <description><![CDATA[<img src='https://i.cbc.ca/ais/90a013d2-70b9-4cd7-b990-c5b5d46ca59b,1792580086104/full/max/0/default.jpg?im=Crop%2Crect%3D%280%2C340%2C1536%2C864%29%3B' alt='A woman and a man hug' width='620' height='349' title='Sabrina Apitz-Grossman, left, and Tomek Apitz-Regan are shown in this undated handout photo.'/><p>Some Ontarians with Stage 4 cancer, whose doctors say they are good candidates for treatments unavailable in the province, were buoyed last month when a man won a court challenge  against his OHIP out-of-country denial. But their hopes were dashed when Health Minister Sylvia Jones responded to the court ruling by changing the rules.</p>]]></description>
